<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nùng people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nùng are a Tai-speaking people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, especially the border provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, closely related to the Tày and to the Zhuang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the 11th century the Nùng chief Nùng Trí Cao rebelled against both Đại Việt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> China. In 1947 the French recognised a Nùng autonomous territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mong-cai">Móng Cái</a>, and many Nùng moved south after 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nung-people.jpg" alt="Nùng people" width="320" height="702" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXLINH · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nung_woman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
